&gt; Great, here you go.<p>As I pointed out, your drop_new is broken for copyable types. For example, consider std::array:<p><pre><code>    auto w = std::array&lt;int, 3&gt;{0, 1, 2};
    drop_new(std::move(w));
    std::cerr &lt;&lt; w[0] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; w[1] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; w[2] &lt;&lt; &#x27;\n&#x27;;
</code></pre>
This prints &quot;0, 1, 2&quot;. Rust&#x27;s drop() doesn&#x27;t suffer from this flaw.<p>&gt; And yes, the compiler will not prevent you from using this value.<p>Yes, <i>that is the point</i>!<p>This bit:<p><pre><code>    std::vector&lt;int&gt; vec {1, 2, 3};
    drop_new(std::move(vec));
    std::cerr &lt;&lt; vec.size() &lt;&lt; &quot; &lt;- ?\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
Simply does not compile in Rust [0]:<p><pre><code>    let vec = vec![1, 2, 3];
    drop(vec);
    println!(&quot;{0} &lt;- ?&quot;, vec.len()); &#x2F;&#x2F; error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: `vec`
</code></pre>
[0]: <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;rust.godbolt.org&#x2F;z&#x2F;GjcMYnEzq" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;rust.godbolt.org&#x2F;z&#x2F;GjcMYnEzq</a><p>&gt; But clang static analyzer will happily detect it.<p>One problem is that you&#x27;re not <i>guaranteed</i> to catch it, similarly to why static analyzers aren&#x27;t guaranteed to catch use-after-frees.
